      {
        "identifier": "https://github.com/MegasKomnenos/parking-unimodality",
        "note": "Registered in Palomar as PALOMAR-2026-11-25-000002, at commit 61ecdf2eb6b2e81ae61fae5df07cae879f3bd157, by the same author. It proves the unimodality of every row as Parking.root_unimodal, whose Lean statement is the one of Parking.unimodal_via_staircase here, over the same definitions. Its route is different: it excludes, in each row, a descent that is followed by an ascent, and it does not determine how the peak of a row stands to the peak of the next row. The present work builds on its library: of the 62 modules of that library, 52 are in this repository under Parking/, with the same code and with the module comments of five of them reworded, and the 28 modules under Staircase/ are new. The staircase is not proved there. One module of that library, Parking/Peak.lean, which is not among the 52 and whose statements are not compared declarations of that entry, contains Parking.row_peak: for n >= 335, row n does not decrease up to a position p, rises strictly from b(n, 81) to b(n, p), satisfies b(n, p+1) <= b(n, p) and falls strictly from b(n, p+1) on, and p lies within a stated distance of order n^(2/3) of the right end of the row. The staircase says more in three respects, since it holds for every n >= 2, is strict from the first entry on, and relates the peaks of consecutive rows, and less in one, since it gives no bound for the position of the peak. No other formalization of either theorem is known.",
        "relationship": "builds-on"
      }
